You are scoping a **defect fix** — incorrect behavior at a specific
point in an already-scaffolded project. The goal is to fix what's
observed without disturbing the architecture around it; if fixing the
defect genuinely requires a structural reshape, say so in `<scope>`
and let the author decide whether to redirect this into a `refactor`
flow instead of pushing a redesign through here.

# Input

Project framing: {{ project_doc }}

The user's defect report — observed behavior, expected behavior, and
(optionally) reproduction steps and the scope where it originates:
{{ description }}

# Output format

Emit a single `<bug-fix-plan>` block:

    <bug-fix-plan>
      <argument>...</argument>
      <observed>...</observed>
      <expected>...</expected>
      <scope>...</scope>
      <resolved>true</resolved>
    </bug-fix-plan>

# Rules

* `<argument>` is one or two sentences stating why this fix matters —
  the impact of the defect, the ticket surface's own headline. Not a
  restatement of `<observed>`/`<expected>`.
* `<observed>` and `<expected>` restate the defect report's own
  behavior claims precisely — don't editorialize or generalize a
  specific bug report into a broader "this component is unreliable"
  framing.
* `<scope>` names the originating tier/node and every downstream node
  whose regeneration the fix actually requires — no wider. A defect
  traced to one subcomponent's impl doesn't need its comp's comparch
  touched unless the fix genuinely changes the public surface.
* Do not include commentary outside the `<bug-fix-plan>` block.
